<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:c="using:JustPlay.UI.Controls"
             x:Class="JustPlay.UI.Controls.BusyOverlay"
             IsHitTestVisible="False">

    <!--
      Shared J.U.S.T. progress overlay - a floating card in a card's OVERLAY layer, so showing
      progress NEVER reflows the layout (Chloe 2026-07-15: an inline progress bar "schiebt unten
      alles rechts raus - das ist schlechtes design. koennen wir kein generelles overlay basteln was
      wir ueberall nutzen koennen?"). Drop it as the LAST child of a card's Panel, bind IsActive to a
      busy flag, Message to a status line and (optionally) Progress to a 0..1 value. It is
      IsHitTestVisible=False - purely visual, it never eats a click on the controls underneath.

      0.6 rebuild (Chloe 2026-07-30: "ich mag die progress anzeige ueberhaupt nicht .. zuckelt zum
      wenn sich der text aendert (es er staendig tut) die bar ist viel zu schmal ... ich haette lieber
      was rundes modernes schickes", then "braucht der progress ne box? dann der ring nicht groesser
      sein?"):

        - ROUND, and it IS the whole thing. A filled disc with the progress arc riding its rim,
          and every line of text living inside it. Nothing is drawn outside the circle, so no
          label can ever land on a list row and turn to mush - which is what the old card, and
          then a scrim, were both compensating for.
        - NO CARD. The bordered pill only ever earned its keep by making TEXT readable over
          arbitrary content; once the text moved inside the disc there was nothing left for it
          to do.
        - IT CANNOT JITTER. The content sits in a FIXED-size panel with the text centred and each
          line at a fixed height, so a message changing per file moves nothing. The original was
          centre-aligned with automatic width - every new string re-measured it and shoved it
          sideways, which is exactly what she saw.
        - The ring's value EASES toward its target (see ProgressRing), so per-file reporting reads
          as one smooth sweep instead of a stutter.

      Anti-flicker is unchanged: nothing appears before ShowDelayMs of continuous busy, so the fast
      per-knob re-renders that make a lab feel instant never flash a spinner.
    -->
    <Panel>
        <Panel x:Name="Pill" IsVisible="False"
               Width="200" Height="200"
               HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center">

            <!-- The disc. Everything the overlay shows lives INSIDE it (Chloe 2026-07-30:
                 "innenflaeche ist gefuellt und beherbergt den text ... dadurch nie overlapping
                 gefahr"), so no text can ever land on a list row and turn to mush - which is what
                 the card, and then the scrim, had been there to prevent. Themed fill + hairline +
                 shadow, so it still reads as a JUST object and tracks the live theme. -->
            <Ellipse Width="188" Height="188" HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                     Fill="{DynamicResource BgLinear}">
                <Ellipse.Effect>
                    <DropShadowEffect Color="Black" BlurRadius="34" OffsetX="0" OffsetY="10" Opacity="0.62"/>
                </Ellipse.Effect>
            </Ellipse>
            <Ellipse Width="188" Height="188" HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                     Fill="{DynamicResource BgGlowA}"/>

            <!-- (!) The rim must sit ENTIRELY on the disc, never across its edge. The track is
                 semi-transparent, so wherever the disc's boundary crosses the band it composites
                 against the disc on one side and the pane on the other - and that shows up as a
                 seam drawn straight through the ring (Chloe 2026-07-30: "da ist eine duenne linie im
                 ring", then "der ring innen - als waere er immer noch kaputt" after I had removed a
                 hairline that was only a second line in the same place). Disc O188 / ring O178 at
                 11 px = band radius 78-89 against disc radius 94: the whole rim lands on filled
                 pixels. Keep that margin if either number ever changes. -->
            <c:ProgressRing x:Name="Ring"
                            Width="178" Height="178"
                            HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                            RingThickness="11" ShowPercent="False"
                            Progress="{Binding $parent[c:BusyOverlay].Progress}"
                            TrackBrush="#1AFFFFFF"
                            ArcBrush="{DynamicResource AccentGradient}"/>

            <!-- Inside the disc: phase on top, the counter below it. Both centred at fixed heights
                 in a fixed-width column - the width is the disc's inscribed square (170/sqrt2 ~ 120),
                 so a longer string trims instead of pushing anything around. -->
            <StackPanel Width="122" Spacing="7"
                        HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                <TextBlock x:Name="MsgText" FontSize="12" Height="17"
                           HorizontalAlignment="Stretch" TextAlignment="Center"
                           Foreground="#C8FFFFFF" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                           Text="{Binding $parent[c:BusyOverlay].Message}"/>

                <TextBlock x:Name="DetailText" FontSize="16" Height="21" FontWeight="SemiBold"
                           HorizontalAlignment="Stretch" TextAlignment="Center"
                           Foreground="#F2FFFFFF" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                           Text="{Binding $parent[c:BusyOverlay].Detail}"
                           IsVisible="{Binding $parent[c:BusyOverlay].Detail,
                                               Converter={x:Static StringConverters.IsNotNullOrEmpty}}"/>
            </StackPanel>
        </Panel>
    </Panel>
</UserControl>
